==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[chock-a-block]]
* [[chock a block]]
* [[choc-a-bloc]]

===Etymology===
{{term|chock}} + {{term|a}} + {{term|block}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-chockablock.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{context|nautical|lang=en}} (of a ship's [[hoist]]ing [[tackle]]) Having the [[block]]s drawn close together so no further [[movement]] is possible, as when the tackle is [[haul]]ed to the utmost.
# {{context|by extension|lang=en}} [[jammed|Jammed]] [[tightly]] together; very [[crowded]]; completely [[filled]] or [[stuffed]].
#: ''Some of Sardinia's gorgeous seaside towns have lately been invaded by builders who erected '''chockablock''' housing that catered to middle-income tourists but threatened to spoil the landscape.''
====Translations====
{{trans-top|jammed tightly together, crowded}}
* Russian: {{t|ru|битком набитый|m|tr=bitkóm nabítyj|sc=Cyrl}}, {{t+|ru|переполненный|m|tr=perepólnennyj|sc=Cyrl}}
{{trans-mid}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# In a [[crowded]] manner; as [[completely]] or [[closely]] as possible.
#: ''His study had books stacked '''chockablock''' on every shelf.''
#: ''The meeting hall was '''chockablock''' full of angry citizens.''
#*'''2012''', The Economist, Oct 13th 2012, ''[http://www.economist.com/node/21564536 Free exchange: Concrete gains]''
#*:AMERICA is full of vast, empty spaces. Europe, by contrast, seems '''chock-a-block''' with humanity, its history shaped by a lack of continental elbowroom. 
